name: kata
description: Arrange supplied content into a clear visual layout, choose a suitable existing form, or retain the current layout. Use when placement, space, grouping, or reading order needs attention. Does not author or rewrite content or infer its internal relationships.
---

# Kata

Kata helps people arrange supplied content so its intended message remains clear. Work with the content as provided: consider its placement, available space, grouping, and reading order. Do not write or rewrite prose, derive relationships, redesign the inside of a supplied chart, table, infographic, code sample, or other content, or invent material to make a layout fit.

The brief and supplied content may state an audience, question, or priority. Use these to assess layout. Do not invent a primary claim or stronger priority. If a consequential layout choice depends on missing intent, ask; otherwise preserve the supplied treatment or give equally important material equal emphasis.

## Choose or retain a form

Supplied content does not need to acquire a Kata pattern to be a valid input. A concise result such as “keep the current layout; it preserves the stated emphasis and remains readable” is complete when no change is needed.

The references describe six existing forms and retain their worked specimens. Those specimens illustrate the catalogue's history and affordances; some demonstrate organizing or authoring information beyond Kata's current supplied-content scope. Do not treat them as authorization to create headlines, classify content, derive relationships, or rewrite source material. Use a form only to arrange supplied material whose relevant relationship and priority are already stated. If the source does not support the relationship, do not infer it.

| Existing form | Supplied relationship or priority | Read |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Aligned comparison | Alternatives are already presented on common criteria | [comparison](references/comparison.md) |
| Whole and parts | Parts and the whole are already identified | [composition](references/composition.md) |
| Nested groups | Membership is already specified | [hierarchy](references/hierarchy.md) |
| Directed dependencies | The entities and edge meaning are already specified | [relationship](references/relationship.md) |
| Time series | A consistently defined measure is supplied at ordered times | [change over time](references/change-over-time.md) |
| Asymmetric composition | The brief supports one item receiving more space beside its context | [golden cut](references/golden-cut.md) |

Read [shared layout assessment](references/layout-assessment.md) for checks that apply across forms, including when to keep the current arrangement. Then read the relevant form reference for its particular constraints. If none fits, explain the layout constraint and what supplied information or intent would change the recommendation. Do not silently shorten text, remove facts, or redesign the content. Preserve any association between content and its caption, source, unit, or qualification.

## Responsibility and output

Kata's current responsibility is the arrangement of supplied content: placement, available space, grouping, and reading order. It does not own content authorship or internal information modeling. The renderer owns output behavior and format; an optional brand skill owns visual identity. See [integration](references/integration.md). For Lys, use the available Lys authoring guidance. Kata works without Lys or a brand skill and does not install either.

Only claim an integration was tested when the actual output has been inspected. Review the rendered result in its intended contexts; structural validity alone does not establish readability. Keep authoring instructions out of the audience-facing content.

The worked examples use fictional data and are specimens, not evidence to reuse in a real presentation. The rationales are design judgments; Gestalt vocabulary supplies analogies, not proof of universal effectiveness. Support specific empirical psychology claims with relevant research and describe its limits.
# Aligned comparison

**Scope:** This reference preserves the existing form and worked specimen.
Use it to arrange supplied comparisons only when common criteria are already
provided. Structure notes describe the specimen's constraints; they do not
authorize deriving criteria, rewriting material, or splitting or hiding content
without the content author's direction. For shared checks, see
[layout assessment](layout-assessment.md).

**Form:** Several alternatives share the same attributes. **Audience question:** Which trade-offs matter for this decision?

An existing matrix accommodates exact values or mixed units; aligned bars or dots can accommodate content centered on one numeric measure. A matrix may be hard to read with long descriptions or unrelated criteria. If supplied material does not fit legibly, state the constraint; split or filter it only when the content author directs that change.

## Structure

In the supplied specimen, alternatives occupy columns and criteria rows, with units in row labels. Identical attributes share positions and precision is consistent. A supplied priority may appear earlier in reading order. Recommendation styling belongs only when it is already supported by the supplied content and brief. Preserve source representations of missing data; do not reinterpret them.

## Retained worked example (historical)

Fictional service launch. An operations lead needs a launch route within six weeks and an initial budget of €80k. Costs are one-time estimates; lead times are estimates too.

| Criterion | Build | Buy | Partner |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Lead time, weeks | 12 | 4 | 6 |
| Initial cost, €k | 120 | 60 | 80 |
| Control | Full | Limited | Shared |

**Before:** The historical example contrasts three paragraphs, each listing its option's time, cost, and control.

**After headline (historical):** “Buy and Partner meet both launch limits”

**After layout:** The historical specimen shows one matrix with constraints above it. It emphasizes two options and adds a trade-off sentence. These are retained example choices, not instructions to rewrite supplied material or assert a priority absent from a user's brief.

## Rationale and limits

Design judgment: common alignment makes a criterion easy to scan. Similarity is a useful Gestalt analogy for repeated cell treatment; it does not prove this matrix is best for every audience. Avoid unrelated icons, ratings without defined scales, and weighted totals without agreed weights.

**Check:** All nine values survive the transformation, both constraints remain visible, and no option becomes a recommendation by styling alone.
# Assessing a layout

Use these checks with any form, including forms not listed in the catalogue. They concern the arrangement of supplied content; they do not ask you to rewrite it or model relationships within it.

## Understand the supplied emphasis

Read enough of the supplied material to avoid misleading emphasis. Identify what it says, which audience or purpose the brief names, and which context, unit, source, or qualification must remain attached for the message to stay accurate. Use only priorities supported by the brief or material. If the intended priority is unclear and would change the layout, ask. If it would not change the layout, retain the supplied treatment or use equal emphasis.

For example, a supplied figure of **8%**, qualified by “among surveyed respondents,” should retain that qualification beside the figure. Giving 8% a dominant field may make it look more general or certain than the supplied evidence supports.

If two supplied claims have equal priority, enlarging one suggests a ranking that the source does not give. Keep them balanced, or ask which one should lead if the brief requires a clear primary message.

## Check the arrangement

- Does size, placement, grouping, or reading order express only the priority supported by the brief?
- Are captions, labels, units, sources, and qualifications readable and clearly associated with the content they explain?
- Are meaningful scales, grouping, and labels preserved? Does the arrangement work at its intended presentation size and phone size when relevant?
- Does the rendered output show clipping or overflow, or text that has become too small to read?
- Does content remain readable without JavaScript and in print where supported? Does the renderer’s keyboard navigation work?
- Would a change resolve a consequential ambiguity, or does the current layout already work?

Keep the current arrangement when it preserves the supplied meaning and remains readable. If space is insufficient, state the constraint for the author. Do not shorten prose, remove facts, invent content, or redesign a supplied item to force it into the layout. Record the sizes and modes reviewed, and note any verification gaps.
# Nested groups

**Scope:** This reference preserves the existing form and worked specimen.
Use it to arrange supplied groups only when membership is already provided.
Structure notes describe the specimen's constraints; they do not authorize
deriving membership, splitting content, or hiding detail without the supplied
author's direction. For shared checks, see [layout assessment](layout-assessment.md).

**Form:** A rooted structure in which each child has one parent. **Audience question:** What belongs where?

The retained examples show nested regions for membership, trees for reporting lines, and indented lists when space is tight. Multiple parents or cross-group dependencies may constrain this form. Do not infer reporting authority from grouping or restructure supplied content to resolve a mismatch.

## Structure

In the specimen, the root and relation are named, siblings have consistent treatment, and children sit within their parent. Spacing distinguishes groups; equal areas avoid implying size. Deeper supplied content may need more space: report the constraint instead of truncating it or moving it to another slide without the content author's direction.

## Retained worked example (historical)

Fictional service launch. A new team member needs the workstream map, not a reporting chart.

- Launch programme
  - Experience: Research, Design
  - Delivery: Engineering, Operations

**Before:** One sentence listing the programme, workstreams, and four disciplines.

**After headline (historical):** “Launch work belongs to two workstreams”

**After layout:** The historical specimen shows an enclosing programme region, with two equal workstream regions and their supplied disciplines. Its annotation clarifies that area does not indicate team size. These are retained example choices, not instructions to derive a hierarchy.

## Rationale and limits

Design judgment: containment expresses membership without a tangle of connectors. Common region is the Gestalt inspiration. Equal region size is a deliberate choice to avoid implying staffing levels. It is not evidence that the workstreams have equal capacity or influence.

**Check:** Each discipline has exactly one parent in the supplied example. Do not silently duplicate a shared team to make the diagram fit.
# Golden cut

**Scope:** This reference preserves the existing form and worked specimen.
Use unequal space only when the supplied brief supports that priority; do not
author a primary claim. For checks shared across forms, see
[layout assessment](layout-assessment.md).

## Message

Give one already-supported message or visual field more room, with a smaller
field for evidence, context, or qualification. This is a compositional choice
about spatial emphasis. It is not a data relationship and does not replace the
pattern needed to compare values, show parts of a whole, represent membership,
dependencies, or change over time.

The conventional 61.8/38.2 split is an optional starting proportion for the
two fields. Adjust it to the content, screen, and reading order; equal or other
proportions may work better. No golden rectangle canvas, phi grid, recursive
subdivision, or particular typography is required.

## Useful when

- The audience has one clear primary takeaway and needs a smaller adjacent
  field for its evidence, source, caveat, or next step.
- A single image, quotation, or concise finding can lead while related context
  remains visible and clearly associated.

For example, a slide may give a service's supported launch date the main field
and put the conditions that qualify it beside the date. Keep the conditions
readable and label the primary statement so the spatial hierarchy does not
overstate certainty.

## Poor fit

- Two claims carry equal importance; making one field larger would imply an
  unsupported priority.
- A dense comparison needs shared row and column alignment for readers to
  compare the same criteria across options. Use a comparison pattern instead.
- The source has no clear primary message, or the medium leaves too little room
  for the secondary field to remain legible.

Adapt by changing the split, stacking fields in reading order for a narrow
screen, or choosing another arrangement for the supplied material. Do not
preserve a side-by-side desktop canvas by shrinking its text until the context
is unreadable. Inspect the rendered phone output and keep the qualification
visible and clearly associated with the primary message. Use a primary message
and relative priority only when the supplied brief supports them. If their
absence makes the layout decision consequential, ask; otherwise retain the
supplied treatment or give the material equal emphasis.

## Keep quantitative meaning separate

The 61.8/38.2 spatial division does not mean that one quantity is 61.8% of a
whole. Do not use it to allocate data, compare unequal categories, encode a
winner, or imply aesthetic or cognitive superiority. Encode actual values with
their supported scales and attach labels to the evidence they describe. State
the source, scope, and uncertainty where they affect interpretation.

For shared checks on supported emphasis, readable context, and whether the
current arrangement should stay, see [layout assessment](layout-assessment.md).

## Worked Lys example

The fictional brief is a supported launch date of 18 June, subject to a
security review due 10 June. The date is the main message; the review is a
qualification that must remain visible. The example uses a flexible main
column and a smaller context column rather than fixing the canvas to a ratio.

```html
<article data-notes="Fictional planning example. The date depends on the security review.">
  <h2>Launch is planned for 18 June</h2>
  <div class="launch-layout">
    <p class="launch-date"><time datetime="2027-06-18">18 June</time></p>
    <aside>
      <h3>Condition</h3>
      <p>Security review due 10 June. The launch date remains planned, not confirmed.</p>
    </aside>
  </div>
</article>
```

The date receives emphasis, while the condition remains explicit beside it.
This is a design judgment about the brief's stated priority, not a claim that
the split improves comprehension.

